Yes, Alpha broke the sort record by a factor of 6.  Note it uses a single
processor (our high end system), with 16 SCSI disks.  The somewhat old
press release is below.  Happens to have been run under VMS however.
				- Jim


           ALPHA AXP SYSTEM PERFORMS WORLD'S FASTEST SORT:
           RUNS INDUSTRY-STANDARD, ONE-MILLION RECORD SORT
                    BENCHMARK IN UNDER 10 SECONDS


       Demonstrates Ability of AXP Systems Running OpenVMS AXP
        Operating System To Process Batch Commercial Workload


MAYNARD, Mass. -- April 6, 1993 -- Digital Equipment Corporation 
today announced that an Alpha AXP system running the OpenVMS 
operating system has shattered a record by performing an industry-
standard sort benchmark in 9.11 seconds -- more than six times 
faster than the supercomputer that set the record last year. 

     The sort benchmark, proposed by a group of 25 database experts 
and first published in DATAMATION magazine, is a standard test of 
batch and utility performance in the database community. Through a
disk-to-disk sort of one million 100-byte records, the sort 
benchmark stresses many facets of a computer system, including 
processor, I/O subsystem, file system, and operating system.

     The previously documented record for the sort benchmark was 
58.3 seconds on a 30-CPU, 30-disk Intel IPSC/2 Hypercube in 1992.  
A CRAY Y-MP supercomputer set an unpublished 26.0-second record.

     "Anyone considering downsizing should look at this benchmark, 
which clearly demonstrates that Alpha AXP systems can exceed 
commercial mainframe performance for a fraction of the cost of a 
mainframe," said William R. Demmer, vice president, Computer Systems 
Group.  "This benchmark also demonstrates the leadership commercial 
functionality of our OpenVMS AXP operating system."

     The sort benchmark was performed by a DEC 10000 AXP system with 
a single, 200-MHz microprocessor and 16 RZ74 SCSI disks.  To set 
this record, the OpenVMS AXP operating system processed 17 input and 
17 output files, read and wrote 100 million bytes of data, and 
performed 25 million in-memory compares, all in under 10 seconds.

     To perform the sort benchmark, Digital created an innovative 
sort algorithm, called AlphaSort (patent pending) to exploit the 
Alpha AXP microprocessor's cache and I/O architecture.  The 
AlphaSort algorithm is designed for modern RISC (reduced instruction 
set computing) architectures typified by very fast processors with 
large caches and large main memories.

     Using an array of industry-standard SCSI disks, the Alpha AXP 
system can read and write data at supercomputer speeds.  The 
AlphaSort algorithm implements a simple file striping scheme to 
assist in balancing Alpha AXP system (processor, cache, and I/O) 
performance.

Background on the Sort Benchmark

     The sort benchmark was first advocated in a 1985 DATAMATION 
magazine article in which a group of 25 database experts from 12 
companies defined three basic benchmarks (debit/credit, scan, and 
sort) to measure the transaction processing performance of computer 
systems. 

The DATAMATION article defined the sort as:

     o  The input is a disk-resident file of one million 100-byte 
        records.

     o  The input file has a 10-byte key field in random order.
  
     o  The output should be a permutation of the input file sorted 
        on the key.

     The sort performance metric is the elapsed time of the 
following seven steps: launch the sort program; open the input file 
and create the output file; read the input file; sort the file; 
write the output file; close the files; and, terminate the program.

     Digital Equipment Corporation, headquartered in Maynard, 
Massachusetts, is the leading worldwide supplier of networked 
computer systems, software and services.  Digital pioneered and 
leads the industry in interactive, distributed and multivendor 
computing.  Digital and its business partners deliver the power to 
use the best integrated solutions - from desktop to data center - 
in open information environments.
