In article <1993Dec28.093351.3036@ivax> imhw400@indyvax.iupui.edu (Mark H. Wood) writes:
>I've been doing distributed processing since the late '70s on DECsystem10,
>DECSYSTEM20, and VAX systems.  Mainframes all, IMHO.  I wonder if Santayana
>ever said anything pithy about those who are not even *aware* of history?

Why would you slander TOPS10 by including it with mainframes?  I would consider
it the prototypical super-mini operating system.  It was user friendly.  Alas,
the machines were too pricey for the coming technology.  We considered buying
them and went with DG's instead so we could distribute the processing across
more platforms.   An idea that is once again becoming popular - as Santayana
would no doubt appreciate.

>Fine.  Don't.  Obviously you've never worked with mechanical engineers.  They
>use more of *everything* -- CPU, memory, *and* I/O -- than you can give them. 
>It ain't *all* transaction processing out there.  Some of us could use *more*
>I/O horsepower per box, because we have single-threaded problems that demand
>it.

I work with Electrical Engineers - I've never had any wind-up ones.
Chip simulation and verification will beat a machine up too.  We want them
bigger and faster and on eveyone's desk with a few extra-big ones in the
computer room for long term batch runs(*).   We can get a lot of them just
by spending the licensing money from the old mainframe.   What we are finding
is that mainframes aren't as big as they think they are.  It may take a lot
of workstations to beat one but we can easily acquire and maintain a lot of
them and, if the workload goes up, we can get more.   Economies of scale
represented by mainframes are fine but the granularity of upgrades is
horrendous - when you outgrow the biggest one what do you do next?

Fiber channel and other I/O technologies will soon rival channel I/O speeds.

Prices are such that the cost of entry is low - encouraging competition - and
you can roll your machines over every few years so the cost of staying current
is also relatively low.  And, if you need a ridiculously large machine, Cray
now offers one based on sooped-up SuperSparcs that is very serious.  And it
runs the same Unix as the system on your desk.  It isn't cheap - about two
years worth of MVS software licensing charges.

This used to be a religious argument but with the current crop of second and
third generation RISC processors and those due in the coming year, the
argument is over.  Take it from a former world-class SuperMini bigot - the
wheel has turned.  John Henry lost.  Steam lost.  That, my friend, is history.

 (*)  the computer room with all the keen UPS and A/C put in to keep the
	  mainframe huffing and puffing along.  It's depreciated - might as
	  well get some honest use out of it.
