I didn't see this exact reference given in this thread, so I thought
I would supply it.  There is a peper by Peter Van Epp and Bill Baines
of Simon Fraser University on the subject of:

"Dropping the Mainframe Without Crushing the Users: 
Mainframe to Distributed Unix in Nine Months".  

The paper was given at LISA VI, Oct 92.  This paper is
online via anon. ftp for those who are interested, via 
the following site/directory:

ftp://ftp.sage.usenix.org:/pub/lisa/lisa6

Your choice of two files:

  vanepp-drop-mainframe.ps   (Postscript, large)

  vanepp-drop-mainframe.txt  (roff, smaller)

Before *everyone* ftp's this paper, please allow me to caution that
it doesn't describe a rosy picture, and some of the information
about mainframe I/O seems vague and misleading.  However, it is
worthwhile reading anyway.

It is interesting to note how a successful project functioned in
moving 18,000 user accounts (which went to 26,000 at the time
the paper was written) to a system with file and compute servers,
with ~all user permanent files on fileservers.  There were (and no
doubt are) a lot of issues involving security, backups, mag tape
support, account management, and operating system performance
(not the Hardware) such as scheduling, etc.  Also noted was an
increase in the amount of system administration staff required,
relative to the mainframe.

