I was rifling through a pile of DEC stuff and came across a DFMSA-AA.  It
has 8 DB-25F connectors (numbered 1 through 8 w/"modem" on 8), a loopback
connector and 4 LEDs, all on the back.  When it is powered on, the LEDs
flash the binary equivalent of the port number that is being looped back.
Also, it appears to listen for DTR on any port, bringing DSR high for
~15 seconds (if nothing is typed) or until a certain quantity of keystrokes
has been sent (depending on the exact key).

Is this some sort of asynch-DECnet routing box?  I've never seen anything
like it before.  It's got an Intel 8031 microcontroller inside w/an EPROM
and RS232 line-drivers as well as several square inches of TTL logic.

