Why the mainframers did not connect SCSI-2 disks to their machines? 
Barriers to entry. IBM mainframe disks were selling at about $7/MB. I buy SCSI-2 
disks at about $0.70/MB. IBM MVS disk device drivers speak a weird protocol 
named Enhanced COunt-Key-Data (ECKD) which assumes the disk can format variable 
length sectors on the fly and can find specially marked 'keys' on the disk.
If IBM changed their drivers to talk to SCSI controllers/disks then they 
could not charge a lot more than what you can buy IBM OEM SCSI disks for 
(e.g. $1/MB at NCA in Santa Clara). Would IBM rather sell disks at $7/MB 
or the same disks at $1/MB.

Of course there is a free market for disk drives and you are perfectly 
free to buy a bunch of SCSI drives and put them in a box that can 
translate from ECKD to SCSI-2 and back. Your box will actually need to 
pretend it is a popular IBM disk controller such as the IBM 3990 and that 
entails accepting all sorts of commands for manipulating controller cache 
and reporting statistics etc. back to the mainframe host. None of this is 
impossible if you know how to do it AND CAN CONVINCE A NUMBER OF 
MAINFRAME CUSTOMERS THAT YOU DID IT RIGHT AND WILL BE IN BUSINESS NEXT 
YEAR to serrvice it all. This exact formula (with much added controller 
cache) is making EMC COrp, Hopkinton, MA fabulously wealthy. Fantastic 
margins help in making good profits.

Barriers to entry such as specialized knowledge and large resource 
requirements can keep margins high for some time.
-- 
