with it. Why do you think Amdahl is going to be selling Suns?

>If you find that you need mainframe I/O for some applications and find other
>apps work better on smaller boxes you could run UNIX on all of the platforms
>making the move from one to the other much easier.

'Mainframe I/O' is a myth, perpetuated by people who either don't understand
the distributed environment or don't have a viable Open Systems product. The
whole point of distrubuted computing is that a properly designed environment
doesn't NEED 'mainframe I/O.' In fact, talking about I/O in those terms sort
of reminds me of people who still thought in assembler when COBOL was the
rage. Not that the understanding isn't required, at some level. But the 
constructs are different between distributed environments and 'mainframe
I/O,' so it's an apples and oranges comparison. The issue today is not
'how many records can I read in a second?' but rather 'can I formulate
the proper data constructs to accomplish all the transactions required
across the enterprise?' So-called mainframe I/O may allow that answer
to be yes in certain cases, without any equivocation, but it also may
mean that LOTS of other cases don't meet the business requirements 
of the departments. 'Mainframe I/O' guarantees that the end-of-day
reports which are supported by a branch of the I/S group run in an
hour. But the transactions that contribute to that report may be 
bottlenecked all day!


>                                               Another advantage to doing
>this is its current compatibilty with syncronous type devices. 

Preserving an investment in 3277s isn't high on most people's list... ...but
even so, if the mainframe system costs more than getting rid of the 3270s,
why do it? How much do dumb terminals cost, in bulk?

>                                                               The system
>supports SNA, TCP/IP, X.25, async terminals, datakit, and local attached 327Xs.

When looking at UTS, one must carefully draw a matrix to see if it's 
possible to get the required features all in the same release. And if
the features will be used, in practice. Except for the 3270 support, 
the forgoing doesn't exactly place UTS in an exclusive club. 


>I have seen this work out nicely for people trying to move in the UNIX direction
>without dumping all their equipment in one move.  This allows for an easier
>migration to UNIX.

This might have been true at one time, when the market-place was different.
However, it doesn't seem to be as good an equation at present. Amdahl seems
to feel the same way, since they have apparently pulled the plug on their
'Mainframe Unix' effort. When you consider their sagging position as a
corporation, such a move is very telling. It's telling me that people 
don't care to pay more for the same thing, where the rubber meets the
road.

>Just a thought,
>Scott
>
>P.S.  The OS I'm referring to is UTS and it's supplied by Amdahl.  There are
>SVR3 and SVR4 versions available.

Again, it would behoove a potential customer to ask some very hard questions
regarding the future of the product and the corporation before shelling out
big bucks and (even more importantly) before getting locked in to getting
things from UTS that they can't get elsewhere.

>* sjp00@amail.amdahl.com          * All opinions stated are my own and do  *
>* Systems Engineer, Amdahl Corp.  * not necessarily reflect those of my    *
>*                                 * employer.                               *
>**************************************************************************** 

I've found that my perspective on the future of large Unix systems has
become a lot clearer since the time when I called that title my own. To
be successful, a vendor must clearly understand its place in the emerging
scheme of things, where it can bring value, and where it can't. Sadly,
some vendors have badly missed the mark and produced really fine products
which have no place in the market.

Greg


