>to eliminate the mainframe, but you could carve out a domain or lpar and install
>the operating system along side your others just to prove your point about UNIX.

We don't need to prove our point about Unix.  We're currently running
a distributed Unix system consisting of roughly 700 workstations (~150
more Alphas sitting in boxes waiting to be deployed).  The point about
Unix has already been made. :-)

What we need to do is get the old beast out the back door since 1)
it's eating us out of house and home with its power and maintenance
costs, and 2) the technology inherant in its design is rapidly aging,
and should probably be replaced if not discarded completely.

Since the main motivation for this move is reducing on-going costs
incurred with keeping the mainframe operational, outfitting it with a
new OS for an extended life-span is exactly the opposite of the
desired result.

The original query was mainly directed at finding a way to provide the
same large amounts of data throughput on a Unix system (part of the
~850 Unix systems on campus) to meet the very specific needs of those
researchers doing jobs with massive data sets.  The monster MVS
mainframe is good at this -- small Unix workstations, in general, are
not.  (Correct me if I'm wrong here, John.)


