Let's look at Digital's current deskside workstation.

Comes with 2 SCSI busses on the motherboard.  Can also take TURBOchannel
adaptors.  Lets presume we use one for a high speed network; e.g. FDDI or ATM.
The motherboard ethernet isn't going to be very interesting for a server
in this class. This leaves 5 slots.  Let's use one more slot for a Presto card.
So now I have 4 slots left.  But wait... We sell a dual SCSI controller.
So I can have 10 SCSI strings on this little, small system.  This translates
to 70 SCSI peripherals (even ignoring RAID systems).  We sell ~4GB disks;
so I can hang > 250GB off of a deskside system; more likely, given the
StorageWorks packages, I'd put it all into a single rack.

And this is just a small "workstation" class system.

Somehow, big dinosaurs just don't make it on my radar screen anymore.

The attack of the killer micros continues...
