Subject: Alpha rom-BOOTP (for tftp-boot) is brain-damaged
Message-ID: <CIB9sq.G1E@news.iastate.edu>
Sender: news@news.iastate.edu (USENET News System)
Organization: Iowa State University, Ames, IA
Date: Mon, 20 Dec 1993 02:05:13 GMT
Lines: 51

I quote from RFC 951, ``BOOTSTRAP PROTOCOL'':

                                                  'secs' is set to the
      number of seconds that have elapsed since the client has started
      booting.  This will let the servers know how long a client has
      been trying.  As the number gets larger, certain servers may feel
      more 'sympathetic' towards a client they don't normally service.
      If a client lacks a suitable clock, it could construct a rough
      estimate using a loop timer.  [...bad suggestion rescinded by
      RFC1542 omitted...]

      Before each retransmission, the client should update the 'secs'
      field.  [and recompute the checksum]

And RFC 1542, ``Clarifications and Extensions for the Bootstrap Protocol'':

      3.2 Definition of the 'secs' field

         The 'secs' field of a BOOTREQUEST message SHOULD represent the
         elapsed time, in seconds, since the client sent its first BOOTREQUEST
         message.  Note that this implies that the 'secs' field of the first
         BOOTREQUEST message SHOULD be set to zero.

         Clients SHOULD NOT set the 'secs' field to a value which is constant
         for all BOOTREQUEST messages.

Having read this I wrote our bootp-server to answer requests after

   0 seconds, if the server is on the requester's subnet
   4 seconds, if the server is on the requester's network
   8 seconds, if the server is not on the requester's network.

so, when we try to tftp-boot our new Alpha it just sits their
dumbly.  Hauling out the packet sniffer, we find it is always
sending the 'secs' field as zero (0), sigh.

So now, I've got to tell our bootp servers that these
hosts are brain-damaged and to answer right away -- just what
I want, *all* our bootp servers answering at once (having a
random delay would mean cross-campus tftp-boots, an even
less appealing vision).

Come on DEC, at least you could just increment the field by
one for each packet...

John
