In article <1993Dec17.175628.3737@ohstpy.mps.ohio-state.edu>,
march@ohstpy.mps.ohio-state.edu (Donald G. Marchlenski) writes:

|>We are looking into telephone voice response/recognitions systems. 

|> Does anyone
|>out there have experience with or knowledge of DIGITAL's DECvoice and
|>DECvoiceBuilder systems? 

   I am rather familiar with the DECvoice product.  (I was an engineer
   on the Digital team that built the DECvoice (DTC04) and the DECvoice
   T1 (DTC05/DTCN5) modules.)

|> The DEC demo at 1-800-DEC-VOIC seemed better than
|>other systems that we have seen (heard).

   The DECvoice allows the programmer to make use of the most appropriate
   technology -- digitization, synthesis, recognition or DTMF -- for the
   particular task at hand.  As a result, the finished applications sound
   quite good...
   
|>Is the voice recognition accuracy good? 

   Yes.  It is also user-programmable for speaker-dependent recognition.

|> Is the application programming fairly
|>simple? 

   The interface is patterned after common VMS system services such as
   $GETJPI, and it allows the programmer to make use of concepts such
   as ASTs. 

   If you have programmed with these features of OpenVMS, the DECvoice
   programming environment should be quite familiar.   

   The DECvoiceBuilder is a higher-level graphical voice application
   development package -- it is a powerful and quick way to build a
   voice application.

   Example DECvoice code is included in the manuals and on the DECvoice
   software ("VOX") distribution kit.

   The call handling capabilities are quite powerfull -- the DECvoice-T1
   (the "multiline DECvoice") telephone module contains a small crosspoint
   switch.


|>   How does the DECtalk unit communicate with the VAX? 

   The DECtalk is a text-to-speech synthesizer with DTMF input capabilities.
   It is available in a standalone enclosure, as a rack mount, as a PC card.
   The DECvoice contains a text-to-speech synthesizer, a voice digitizer, a
   voice recognizer.  (It also contains various other capabilities such as
   digitized concatentation and language-sensitive name synthesis routines.)
   The DECvoice is available as a Q-bus module.

   The (external) DECtalk synthesizers communicate via serial a ASCII
   protocol comprised of ANSI escape sequences.  The DECtalk can be
   connected to and operated from any host capable of communicating
   via serial ANSI escape sequences.  Example DECtalk code is available
   from DECUS.  OpenVMS hosts contain a run-time library intended to
   assist DECtalk programmers, though OpenVMS programmers can also use
   the ANSI escape sequence interface to the DECtalk.

   The DECvoice is a quad-Q-bus module and is integrated in more closely
   to a host VAX or MicroVAX bus due to the data rates involved with
   recognition and digitization technologies.  (8 simultaneous calls
   using the digitization capabilities of a single DECvoice-T1 module
   can create an aggregate 128 kilobits per second data rate or more...)

