In article <CIAG8s.62C@news.iastate.edu>,
john@iastate.edu (John Hascall) asks about mainframe replacement
hardware solutions.

I work in a shop that's been doing (fairly) large-scale DP
on UNIX boxes since the early 80's.

We've dealt with the same kinds of problems you're probably facing now
for _quite_some_time_.  We've have had lots of frustrations along the way,
but ultimately things worked out _really_well_.

As others have said, the UNIX big-iron market is really tiny in the
eyes of the iron vendors (though it's much larger than they think,
since each sale can be in the hundreds-of-thousands if not millions
of dollars, even though there aren't many of them).

>     * Disk systems faster than SCSI (IPI?  Raid?  ???)

We're moving away from IPI in favor of SCSI.  It's less expensive,
more reliable, and easier to get.  With fast/wide/differential SCSI,
speed and cable distance no longer problems.

As for Raid, it's _always_ implemented on SCSI (at least the hardware
solutions), though it can gain you sustained performance that's higher
than what individual disks can get you.

>     * 3480 cartridge tape devices whose speed is
>       similar to the mainframe versions AND which
>       are robust enough for hundreds of tape operations
>       a day (insertions/deinsertions)

We use Fujitsu 3480 drives for just this purpose.
I know we've gotten them to work properly on Solbournes (since 1991)
and SGI's (just a few weeks ago).

I also know we _almost_ got them working on Suns, but I don't know if
they work now or not (the SGI driver was only recently made to work properly).

IBM and NCR claim to have IBM 3480/3490 drives working, but we haven't
tested them.

I don't think DEC supports 3480 at all.

I'm not the resident 3480 expert, so take this with a grain of salt.


>     * 9-track tapes drives with the similar speed
>       and robustness.

We use the HP's drives mentioned by others, but they're pretty sorry
compared to the monster 9-track drives on our old VAXen (since departed).
Even those couldn't compare to the 3480's.
I'd get out of the 9-track business if I were you.

>     * also information on silo technology (either 3480
>       or other media).   We currently have some 2000
>       3480 cartridges (probably 90+% of the requests
>       could be satisfied with a 500-unit silo though,
>       leaving the rest for the operator).

We've got a StorageTek silo that holds 6000 tapes (we're closing in on
around 100,000 total).  Smaller versions are also available.
Hardware-wise, it works flawlessly (we did have a long
shake-down period, but we _were_ the _only_ customer of
the SCSI/UNIX version for a few months...)

The real problem with these things is software.

There are commercial systems out there, but they didn't meet our
needs, so we rolled our own (you can read about my part in it in the
Winter 1993 USENIX Procedings).  Others who have rolled their own
include CERN (see the Summer 1992 USENIX Procedings)
and Berkeley University (see the Winter 1993 USENIX Procedings).

>	We also
>       have about 700 4mm DAT tapes we use currently
>       for backing up our Unix system currently.

I don't know who's making 4mm silos, but I've heard rumours that the
StorageTek silos can handle them.

