In article <JOHNS.93Dec20090922@haiti.gsfc.nasa.gov>, johns@haiti.gsfc.nasa.gov (Steve Johns) writes:
|>We just got an Alpha 3000 running OSF1, and it's method of starting up
|>is quite a bit different from Ultrix 4.3, which I am used to.
|>
|>Specifically, I would like advice on the best place/method for calling
|>scripts/commands which I want executed during startup - the things I
|>used to run from rc.local.  All this multiple run level stuff with
|>starts and kills is confusing what used to be a very simple process
|>(though I can see the flexibility here...)
|>
|>For example, I need to do a "route add default <address>" command to
|>ensure that a default route is defined in my routing table every time
|>I boot.  Where should I put this command?  (Also, is there another way
|>to get the system to do define this itself through one of the setup
|>programs?)

Create/edit a file called /etc/routes and place into one or more lines 
of the form:

net	gateway

(For instance you could put in

default	wan-gw

which I what I use for my system).

But the general solution is to create /bin/sh (not csh or bash or ...)
and place it in /sbin/init.d ; the script takes one argument
which will be either start and stop.  Then in /sbin/rc3.d create a
symlink to it and have the start with Snn where nn is an appropriate
number for where/when it should be executed.  Once you see the scheme
used it's pretty to get your stuff in there. 
