We have been following this technology (3480) closely as we have invested
a lot in the technology.  We have 3480 drives on our VAX, SGI and DG
Aviion servers.  We also have a STK 4400 Silo.  We are in the process of
rolling much of our 45 Tb archive over to 3480 so we really appreciate
the reliability and high transfer rates.  We are looking forward to the
STK helical scan drives as it would increase our Silo capacity from 1.2 Tb
to 120 Tb.  Supposedly their implementation of helical scan will provide
high tranfer rates, fast positioning and little or no contact between the
head and tape.  The cartridges will be mirror image with the notch in the
other corner (so the robotics can tell the difference).  It also sounds
like they will be increasing transfer rates in the near future.

Whether or not the tapes can be intermixed in the silo depends on what
software package you use and whether it is smart enough to put the tapes
in the right type of drive.  I do not believe that Unitree is capable of
this currently.

We did not consider 8mm or 4mm technologies for our archives due to shelf
life and wear considerations.  We still use them for data exchange, data
distribution and backups though.  If you go with Exabyte technology, use
data rated tapes to avoid gumming up the heads and buy the newer and more
reliable half-height drives.

