In article <1993Dec20.150630.1633@ulysses.att.com> cjc@ulysses.att.com (Chris Calabrese) writes:
>In article <CIAG8s.62C@news.iastate.edu>,
>john@iastate.edu (John Hascall) asks about mainframe replacement
>hardware solutions.
>
>>     * Disk systems faster than SCSI (IPI?  Raid?  ???)
>
>We're moving away from IPI in favor of SCSI.  It's less expensive,
>more reliable, and easier to get.  With fast/wide/differential SCSI,
>speed and cable distance no longer problems.

Being an IPI bigot, I have to put in my 2 cents worth in an effort to
quell misinformation :-).

Certainly SCSI is slightly less expensive than IPI, but it is not
more reliable or easier to get (especailly if you are talking about
fast/wide/differential).  And the data rates have a distance to go
before being comparable to IPI.  The extra expense may be well worth
the difference in performance.

Current IPI drives can sustain an average of about 10MB/sec data
transfers (Elite-3) compared to about half of that for SCSI drives.
(I'm talking about the media rate here, not the rate out of the
buffers in the electronics...the media rate is important for
transfers of any substantial size).  This is because IPI drives
typically use 2 heads (or more) in parallel.  Even though FWD SCSI
has a 20MB/sec spec, that is only for the data transfers.  The command
packets are done at the old 5MB/sec slow narrow speeds.  This makes
the overhead for FWD SCSI very high, it can be as high as 30%.
IPI does not have this drawback and is very efficient.

As for reliability, SCSI computes the ECC, CRC, whatever, at the
drive, so any noise on the cables that changes bits has little
chance of being detected.  IPI computes the error detection at the
controller that typically plugs into the computer bus.  This helps
detect and correct errors due to noise on the cables...important for
very long differential cables (the standard for IPI).  Aside from
that, the IPI drives use exactly the same heads, media, and
mechanics as the SCSI drives with only the electronics a little
different, so there is no inherent reliability problem with IPI that
does not also exist for SCSI.  IPI drives also have true dual
porting as a standard feature so that high availability systems can
be easily implemented for additional reliability.
>
>As for Raid, it's _always_ implemented on SCSI (at least the hardware
>solutions), though it can gain you sustained performance that's higher
>than what individual disks can get you.
>
???ALWAYS??? You mean if I go out and buy any SCSI controller and
drive, it will have RAID implemented??  !!!NOT!!!  Sure, there are
RAID boxes with SCSI interfaces, but not consistent with your
argument that SCSI is less expensive than IPI.

The IPI and fwdSCSI controllers that our company manufactures for
TURBOchannel, Futurebus+, SBus (and soon PCI) have RAID 0 and
RAID 1 built into them as a standard feature.  These can EASILY
attain 16-18MB/sec transfer rates from the I/O subsystem to the
CPU memory by striping two drives on a controller.  It would probably
take twice as many fast/wide SCSI drives and controllers to attain
that data rate (now tell me again, which is more expensivei? :-).

I know that SCSI and IPI technologies will ultimately converge in
performance, but by then both will probably take a back seat to newer
and faster technologies like fibrechannel.

