Message-ID: <1993Dec21.203905.1@cc.helsinki.fi>
NNTP-Posting-Host: hylka.helsinki.fi

We have a DEC Alpha 4000/610 running OSF/1 V1.3 in an ethernet. We also
have an old HP-9000 running HP-UX 7.0 in another ethernet. The two
ethernets are connected via two PCs running KA9Q 920603 acting as a
router - the two PCs are connected via modems and the protocol used is
SLIP.

We have a PC running OS/2 1.3 and PC/TCP for OS/2 in the same ethernet
as the Alpha.

THE PROBLEM IS: IN SOME SITUATIONS, THE ALPHA CANNOT SET UP A NEW SOCKET
CONNECTION. When I look with netstat, I can see the socket hanging in
SYN_SENT state until a timeout clears it.

For example, when I start a ftp session from HP to Alpha, I can log into
the Alpha and give the CD command, but when I try a GET or DIR command,
after a long time I get an error message. When I issue a dir command,
netstat on HP shows a new socket in LISTEN state and netstat on Alpha
shows a new socket in SYN_SENT state.

I can't even make a ftp session from Alpha to HP, all I get is a
timeout. Before the timeout, I can see the ftp socket in SYN_SENT state.
I can ftp from the OS/2 machine to the HP without problems.

I can initiate a rcp transfer (both directions) from HP to Alpha, but
not vice versa.

I can't mail from Alpha to HP. I haven't tried vice versa, and I don't
recall exact symptoms right now.

I can't use (via the LPD protocol) from Alpha a remote printer which is
attached in the HP.

I can telnet from HP to Alpha but not vice versa.

Ping works well either way.

A month ago the Alpha was in the same ethernet as the HP; then LPD, ftp
and mail worked all right. This would indicate that there's something
wrong with the SLIP routers, but the OS/2 machine can use telnet and ftp
OK to the HP via the SLIP routers. So the problem must be the
combination of DEC Alpha and KA9Q routers.

What's so special with OSF/1 TCP/IP implementation? Is it just so new
and KA9Q so old that they don't understand each other? Or is it
something entirely different?

I have played with /etc/iptos: tried without it, tried to use values 0x0
and 0x10 for both the control and data sockets, but no luck.

How should I debug this further?
--
Juhani Jaakola, jaakola@cc.helsinki.fi


