  All of the available Unix versions (that I know of) for the VAX series of
systems require a license - there isn't any "free" version like there are for
the PC systems.

  Before you pursue this further, you might want to consider the performance
gains you will get with a PC-based system. We replaced a VAX-11/785 w/ 32Mb
of memory (running 4.4BSD-alpha) with a 386DX/25 (running BSD/386 0.9.4) and
saw a substantial performance improvement, not to mention the space and power
savings 8-)

  We subsequently replaced some VAX 8650's with a 486DX2/66, and plan to con-
tinue this migration.

> 	On a similar note, can anyone suggest the same for a historical
> artifact like VMS V3???   DECUS would be my first guess, but they seem to be
> in a total uproar this decade, and the phone contact we had with them yielded
> a listing of the RT-11 C compilers and utilities that they could supply.

  The DECUS library only gets DEC products when the entire product is discon-
tinued, not just one version. Thus, they won't have old versions of VMS. The
normal DEC software license lets you "run the current version, or any previous
version", so any VMS license should do. However, DEC still wants a lot of money
(too much, considering their competition's pricing) for VMS licenses. The
cheapest way to get a "legitimate" VMS license is to buy a complete system w/
VMS properly licensed, and then send the license transfer paperwork and a check
for $300 to DEC. [This is the deal here in the US - it may vary in other areas
of the world].

