In article <1993Dec13.151455.16402@desire.wright.edu>, 
				ejones@desire.wright.edu (Ed Jones) writes:
> First, let me say I know very little about VMS as I come from a unix
> background. :)  I have a friend who just bought an old Wyse 50 
> terminal that he would like to use to dial into the VAX here on
> campus. It easily works on our unix boxes since we have termcap
> entries for this terminal. But, VMS cant figure this terminal
> out. I tried a set/term/inquire but it saud unknown terminal
> type. So, has anyone out there found a way to get this terminal
> up and running with VMS? Is there a set of paramaeters I can use
> with set/term to get this beast a'workin? Thanks for the newbie
> help!  :)

        First of all,  the  DCL  command  $  SET  TERMINAL /INQUIRE will
    _only_  recognize  DEC  terminal types (VTxxx),  or  terminals  that
    return a VTxxx identification string (or whatever you call it).   So
    the  Wyse 50 will NOT be recognized on a SET TERM/INQ unless you can
    make it act like, say, a VT100.

        However, a lot of  terminals  do  include  one or more DEC VTxxx
    emulations.  You might check in the way to setup the Wyse to emulate
    a VT100 or VT220 mode of operation.  If so, don't do a SET TERM/INQ,
    but instead do a $ SET TERM /DEVICE=VT220 or /DEVI=VT100, which ever
    is appropriate.

        Finally, even if the Wyse does  NOT do a strict VTxxx emulation,
    it  really  only  needs to do a subset.  For example, a  minimum  of
    capability would be responding to ANSI escape sequences.  That  much
    will  allow  you  to use DEC editors, etc.  If this is the case, you
    (or your  system  manager)  can  create  the  VMS  equivalent  of  a
    "termcap"  entry for the Wyse which _names_ the terminal type,  e.g^
    "Wyse50", and defines its capabilities for VMS,  e.g^  ANSI_CRT  and
    perhaps DEC_CRT, etc.  This know as making a "TERMTABLE" entry.  The
    Screen  Management  (SMG$)  Utility manual describes the steps to go
    through.  Examples for  DEC  terminals  can  be found in SYS$SYSTEM:
    SMGTERMS.TXT.   You can make such a termtable for your private  use,
    or it can go into a table that is made available system-wide by  the
    system  manager.   If  you  would  like  more  details on creating a
    termtable entry, drop me a note via e-mail.

        Oh, and even if the Wyse  does  emulate a VT100, it may still be
    beneficial  to  create  a  termtable  entry  named  "Wyse50",  which
    basically duplicates the  VT100  entry  in  SYS$SYSTEM:SMGTERMS.TXT,
    because  then  a user can do $ SET TERM/DEV=WYSE50 at login, and VMS
    will  recognize   that   "device"   type   and   set   the  terminal
    characteristics appropriately.

        -Ken
