In article <karasawa.755923438@aseu02>, karasawa@fuchinobe.skk.slb.com (KARASAWA) writes:

>Does anyone out there know how to set up TLZ06-DA tabletop model to have
>storage capacity up to 4.0GB?

The 4 GB capacity is dependent on the effectiveness of the compression
algorithm in the tape drive.  If you have data that doesn't compress
well or is already compressed, you won't get that kind of capacity.

>I know I have to specify 61000bpi for density
>and 114880 for length, but it doesn't seem to me that it holds that much 
>capacity.

The length and density information in dump can be used to get
good *estimates* of the amount of tape that dump will try to
use.  If you don't use an explicit length and density dump will
always write to the of the tape, even though the estimates may
be off.  Versions of ULTRIX and OSF that support the TLZ06 will
have the correct values to make reasonable estimates.  Versions
that don't support the TLZ06 will assume it is a 9 track 1600
bpi tape and give poor estimates.

BUT!  When you use explicit length and density information, dump
will trust you and only use as much as it estimates will be
used.

Finally, it is hard for dump to estimate how well data is going
to compress...
 
>Since other model such as TLZ06-AA/BA have SCSI switch to set up
>SCSI ID and compression enable/disable switch, the box I have might have switch
>somewhere internally, but I couldn't find it.

Versions of ULTRIX and OSF that support the TLZ06 allow selection of
compression vs. non-compression by the tape special device file
used.  See the previous note directing you to the "tz" manual page.
See also; the manual page on mtio.

