The device depends on the SCSI ID of the floppy disk drive. On my RX26, the
SCSI ID switch is under the floppy disk drive (which is mounted internally).

If you don't know the SCSI ID, typing 'SHOW DEV' in the monitor should
give you a list of all the SCSI devices:

If the floppy disk drive is mounted internally, then it will be on
SCSI bus A and the corresponding device will be /dev/rz%c (where % the
SCSI ID). If it is an external drive, it is on SCSI bus B and you have
to add 8 to the SCSI ID to get %.

For instance, I have an external RRD40 CD-ROM at SCSI ID 2. Its
corresponding device is /dev/rz10c. My internal floppy drive is at
SCSI ID 5 so its device is /dev/rz5c.

Note that OSF doesn't seem to automatically create devices in /dev. So
when you've figured out your drive's SCSI ID, you have to run MAKEDEV.
For instance I had t0 run './MAKEDEV rz5' in my /dev directory to
create the rz5c device.

I hope this gets you going, since I still haven't tried mtools...

