I had held off replying initially to this because I did not know just what
was being referred to.   Now that I do, I can help explain the state of things.

On DEC OSF/1 (as of V1.3) there are actually two C compilers shipped with the
system.  The one you get with the plain cc command uses a code generator
based on MIPS compiler technology.  This compiler uses uopt for its 
optimization.  The other compiler is accessed with cc -migrate; this uses
the common DEC C front-end (same as on other Digital platforms) along with our
GEM back-end/optimizer.  We generally recommend that you use cc -migrate if
you're looking for the best performance.  (I know the natural question is "Why
are there two C compilers?"  This is something I don't have an answer for.)

The current C++ on DEC OSF/1 uses the common DEC C++ front-end combined with
the MIPS code generator.  I'm not too astonished that uopt doesn't work with
it, though.  We do have plans to replace the MIPS code generator in DEC C++
with GEM, but I don't know when that will happen.  (Note that DEC C++ on our
other Alpha AXP platforms is GEM-based.)

All other Digital compilers for DEC OSF/1 (Fortran, Ada, Pascal, etc.) are
GEM-based and should be quite reliable as well as offering superior performance.
Digital's quoted benchmark numbers are generated using the GEM-based compilers
(cc -migrate and DEC Fortran).

I've spoken with a number of customers who are using DEC OSF/1 for major
applications, and they all are impressed with the quality and performance of
the system and its compilers.
