Here is the summary of replies I received to my request for help with getting
data from a PDP running RSX (with 8" RX02 floppies) onto a PC. Sorry for the
delay - I've been confined to bed with the Dreaded Lurgie!

Many thanks to all who replied to my enquiries - this is the first time I've
really attempted to tap the net-expertise out there, and I'm impressed with
the response I've had. Much appreciated.

			+	+	+

|> Maybe this is a FAQ, but I've not managed to find it anywhere. Let me know
|> if I've missed it, or if there's a better group I should be posting to.

Charles Lasner (cjl@com.maestro):

"Yes, post it to alt.sys.pdp8"

[I'll try this, and I'll post an update of any new suggestions]

|> (i)	Can I interface an 8" floppy drive to a standard PC controller on
|> 	a PC clone computer? Do I simply need a conversion cable?

Tony Duell (ard@siva.bris.ac.uk):

"Yes, but not to read RX02's. The IBM 1.2Mbyte disk is very similar to an 8"
disk (it rotates at the same speed - 360 RPM for example), and it's trivial to
link the right signals up. I've done it several times. Now, the problem is in
data density. The original IBM PC controller could _only_ do double density,
and many clones are similar. Even those that claim single density operation are
not often reliable.
The RX01 is a standard Single-density disk. The RX02 is a very non-standard
Double density disk, with some bits of the track, like the sector headers,
being written single density I think. Nothing but a DEC RX02 drive will read an
RX02 disk, unless it was specifically designed to do it. RX01's can be read on
standard single density controllers, and can be read/written on RX02 drives"

James Lothian (jlothian@uk.ac.ed.castle):

"I *think* this is do-able; a bloke I used to work with put an 8"
floppy on his PC and hacked up some horrible software that enabled
him to read the 8" floppies off my 11/40 (rt11 format). Unfortunately
I don't know where he is now.

[later message]
As a followup to the message that Tony Duell posted,
I should mention that the bloke I mentioned who read 
RX02 floppies on his PC was only reading single-density
ones. As far as I can tell, double-density ones are a 
total no-no." 

Bruce Mitchell (WNIMHACK@EDU.MSUS.Winona.VAX2):

"You can do just about anything if you have enough time and money.  This
would require a lot of both."

Ken Yap (ken@au.csiro.dit.syd):

"I forget how large the RX02 was but I think it's high density (77
track, 600k or 1.2M depending on whether it is SS or DS). You would
need a special cable to go from 50 pins to 34 pins.  And the PC
controller would have to be a HD one. The other problem is that I think
the RX02 block size is 256 bytes."

Charles Lasner (cjl@com.maestro):

"Yes you can, but it gives you an MFM 8" Floppy, not an FM one.  I remember
reading years ago an article where someone claimed that making small
changes to an XT floppy controller would allow it to read FM disks like
RX01.  No claim for writing was made, and I don't find it credible that
the change would be innocuous to its normal MFM read/writing/formatting. 
Moreover, this is a 250 KHz controller only.  Most 8" floppies are 500 KHz
although the motor speed is 20% faster (360 RPM not 300 RPM) so the data
packing density is 20% short of being double the DSDD norm, etc. 
Moreover, the article doesn't address the issue of which particular XT
controller; the author is ignorant of the fact that there are multiple
types, and he was referring to a clone!  In any case, many early floppy
controllers provide an external connector for this purpose.  It allowed
compatibility with earlier prototype machines that became the IBM PC but
used the 8" disks only.  Why the connector remained on the production
boards is a moot point; most people never used it."



|> 
|> (ii)	Is there any software around (PD/commercial) which would allow me
|> 	to read the RX02 format (written on an RSX system, so I guess this
|> 	implies Files-11)? Do some PC BIOSs include 8" floppy support, and
|> 	would this be any help to me (since it isn't an MSDOS floppy!)


Tony Duell (ard@siva.bris.ac.uk):

"Not that I know of. You may do better to use FLX on the RSX system (I think)."
 
Bruce Mitchell (WNIMHACK@EDU.MSUS.Winona.VAX2):

"No."

Ken Yap (ken@au.csiro.dit.syd):

"You'd have to program the floppy controller yourself. File structure
all different. Even the recording may be inverted."

Charles Lasner (cjl@com.maestro):

"Hopeless.  Support of external MFM disks would be user-written anyway;
forget the BIOS.  But more importantly, there is no support for FM disks
(RX01) and DEC's DD RX02 format is *not* MFM but in fact an incompatible
variant no other drives can read.  Again, in theory, if the changes cited
allow the 765 chip to work for FM, which the chip is rated for, *then* in
theory you could write support for RX01 yourself, including something like
FILES-11 or RT-11 data structure on top of the basic physical RX01 (3740)
support.  But RX02 is impossible!"



|> 
|> (iii)	Alternatively, would it be possible for the RX02 controller on the
|> 	RSX host to control a 3.5" or 5.25" drive, so that only a software
|> 	driver would be required to read it on the PC? (ie. could the
|> 	RX02 controller drive, say, a 1.44MB 3.5" drive, using only 77
|> 	tracks instead of 80, and using 26 256-byte sectors instead of
|> 	18 512-byte ones?

Tony Duell (ard@siva.bris.ac.uk):

"No Way!. The RX02 'controller' is a simple bus adaptor, which sends commands
serially to the drive. The drive contains a lot of AMD bit-slice chips and
enough RAM to buffer a sector. You tell the drive to copy a sector into it's
RAM, and then to transfer the RAM to the host machine
The Drives inside the RX02 communicate with the CPU board via a special
read/write board. It's not trivial (I'll not say impossible, but I am not going
to design it!) to use any other drives with the controller"

James Lothian (jlothian@uk.ac.ed.castle):

"Unfortunately, if what you've got is a genuine DEC RX02, the interface
between the controller and the dual drive assembly is entirely proprietary
to DEC and not any kind of standard, so the RX02 controller isn't any use
with any other kind of floppy. It used to be possible to get hold of 
third-party RX02 emulators, mainly for QBUS systems, that had a standard
Shugart interface and could (in theory) be used with other sizes of floppy.
I don't know if there were ever any Unibus versions of these. "

Bruce Mitchell (WNIMHACK@EDU.MSUS.Winona.VAX2):

"No, even though some people have *claimed* it can be done, nobody has
ever done it that I know of."

Ken Yap (ken@au.csiro.dit.syd):

"This is probably more feasible since a HD is 80 tracks. I know for
example some people have attached HD drives to a Tandy 6000 with HD 8"
drives.  The hardware info is at plains.nodak.edu in the Tandy
directory."

Charles Lasner (cjl@com.maestro):

"On some DEC systems maybe.  In any case, they all would already be
supporting RX50 which can be dealt with directly on the PC with existing
software."



|> 
|> (iv)	Any other suggestions?

Tony Duell (ard@siva.bris.ac.uk):

"Yes. How about building a card for the PC that simulates the serial data link
of the RX02 bus adaptor, and then linking a real RX02 up to the PC?"

Art Hurst (ahhurst@com.mmm):

	"Your best bet is to install Kermit on the 11/60 and your Sun and
	transfer files that way via RS232. That way you will only deal
	with software and could likely automate the process via
	scripting on the sun. I used Kermit between an 11/73 and an
	11/70 for two years as a poor man's DecNet. It only got screwed
	up when the power failed. Trying to interface an RX02 and read
	FILES11 format would be a nightmare!  You can get copies of
	Kermit via ftp from Columbia."

James Lothian (jlothian@uk.ac.ed.castle):

"It might be worthwhile to investigate the possibilities of getting
(say) RX50s (5 1/4" floppies) for the microvax. There's bound to be
some public domain software to let a PC read an RX50 floppy in some
sensible format. Transfer your data from RX02 to RX50 on the microvax,
then read it on the PC. It's a bit messy, though. You used to be able
to get RX50s for Unibus too, but if your 11/60 is really topped out,
that might not be any use."

Bruce Mitchell (WNIMHACK@EDU.MSUS.Winona.VAX2):

"Yes.  Get a copy of RSX Kermit and run it through a 9600 baud modem.
This gives you an effective transfer rate of 4800 binary blocks per hour,
or about 6 RX02s per hour.  Even at 1200 baud you can move about 1 RX02
per hour.

Or.  Get an RUX50 controller which controls RX50 5.25" for the Unibus,
and then write RT-11 format RX50s using the RSX FLX utility.  This format
can be read by a PC using a shareware utility.  You will have to format
your RX50s on the PC using another shareware utility."

John Lees (lees@cps.msu.edu):

"I suggest you forget about the disks and use either a serial or
parallel connection to transfer the data. Used boards are dirt
cheap for the PDP-11, and everything is dirt cheap for the PC.
On RX02 floppies you're not really talking about transferring
that much information. A 19200 baud serial line should do. If
you really need speed, do a 16-bit parallel transfer (DLV11 in
the PDP-11).

Heck, tear out the floppy drive and have the application dump
its data to a cheapo PC for logging to a 3.5" diskette. Anything
would be better than dinking around with RX02 floppies at this
point in time!"

Ken Yap (ken@au.csiro.dit.syd):

"Sure you can't set up a serial line into the 11/60 or something? Much
less hassle even if slower and you have to pull out a card or two?"

Charles Lasner (cjl@com.maestro):

"Expensive.  Shaftstall sells a device which reads all sorts of file
formats.  It includes its own 8" drive and supports RX01 and RX02 directly.

Get a faster serial line to the PC and use Kermit."

David Koltenbah (00dekoltenba@edu.bsu.bsuvc.leo):

"Contact,     MicroSolutions Computer Products,
              132 W. Lincoln Hwy.,
              DeKalb, IL 60115
              USA
              Phone:  815-756-3411
              FAX:    815-756-2928
and inquire about their CompatiCard floppy controller and their Uniform
drivers for floppy drives, including 8" drives in various formats."

