In article <1993Dec6.162805.24381@cm.cf.ac.uk>,
scocpb@thor.cf.ac.uk (Chris Brown) writes:
Chris> Using xmosaic (v1.2) or mosaic (2.0) on a DECstation 5000/25
Chris> results in the following warnings:

Chris> "-adobe-times-medium-r-normal-*-17-*-*-*-*-*-iso8859-1" [...]

Chris> In the case of v1.2 I altered the resources so as to use fonts
Chris> I knew we had. I would rather install the font-set as used by
Chris> NCSA but don't know how to go about obtaining the above fonts.

Mosaic uses a mixture of pixel sizes that, with the standard MIT X11
font distributions, can only be satisfied by mixing 75dpi and 100dpi
fonts.  DECstations by default only load the font set appropriate for
the current display, resulting in missing fonts.  If you have both
the 75dpi and 100dpi fonts on disk, you can add fonts to search path
with the "xset" command in /usr/bin/X11 (if you loaded the unsupported
MIT X11 subsets) or with an "fp+" switch on the X startup line in
/etc/ttys.

I complained to MarcA about this back around xmosaic 0.5 or so, but
it never did get changed.

-dan
