In article <oxenreid.755298294@chaos.cs.umn.edu>, oxenreid@chaos.cs.umn.edu () writes:
> The problem is this:  We run the computer in the shop for days, and
> we have no trouble, but when we place it in the hut at the scales, it
> does a major crash on the disk within days.  Our present solution is to reload
> the OS after a crash.

  What do you mean by "crash"? Physical disk failure, or corruption of the 
disk's contents, or...? Do you get any messages on the console?

> I know how to start a maintenance shell on an RS6K, 
> but I have no information on how to do it for the PDP-11.  We have sent the 
> unit in to Bell Atlantic, but they found no problem.  They think (as do I) 
> that it is in the UPS (a sola ferroUPS).  The other technician thinks that it 
> is the disk controller.  Bell Atlantic wants to sell us a drive controller 
> for $1700.00  this is a bit steep for me to accept.  We are going to get a 
> power line monitor in about a year, but that does not help me now.   BTW, 
> the PDP-11/23 is running a "KEF11-A microcode option" and RT-11.

  Is the "shop" environment identical to the "hut" environment? Temperature,
humidity, power, etc.? What is different? Note that power problems shouldn't
consistently corrupt the disk contents. However, if you think it's the UPS,
then take the UPS out and see if it helps (or if the problem symptoms change).

> 1)  How to start a maintenance shell and do a crash recovery or 
>     analysis (if such a thing exists).

  Diagnostics don't run under the operating system on PDP-11's. There is an
outboard diagnostic package called XXDP+ that is used for testing. The CPU
board (assuming it's an 11/23-Plus) has on-board diagnostics for CPU and mem-
ory. You'd normally boot XXDP+ from tape or another disk drive.

> 2)  Can I upgrade the PDP-11/23 to some other PDP-11/XX by moving some of the
>     ROM information or CPU to a new system?  [Where XX is >23]

  You can upgrade from the 11/23 to the 11/53, 73, 83, or 93 by replacing the
CPU module (and possibly some memory and/or boot cards, depending on what you
have and what you want). Depending on what software you're running, changes
might be needed.

> 3)  Who else sells PDP-11 drive controllers (so that I can prove a point)?

  Lots of people. $1700 is rather steep. Consider that you can buy a used
RQDX3 controller (ST-506 interface) for under $250 and a drive for about the
same amount. What sort of disk and controller do you have now? A brand new
CMD controller only costs about $1000 and you can get a nice 500Mb SCSI disk
drive to go with it for $700 or less.

> 4)  Is there a FAQ for these questions?

  Probably not.

> 5)  What sort of general information on PDP hardware should I know?

  Your best bet would be to get someone local who knows PDP-11's to come out
and discuss your options with you. Other than that, send me email and we can
discuss your configuration in more detail.

