In article <9312081236.AA13326@biomed.abdn.ac.uk>, george@bio-medical-physics.aberdeen.ac.uk (George Cameron) writes...
>[Posted to comp.ibm.pc.hardware, comp.sys.dec, comp.sys.dec.micro]
> 
>Maybe this is a FAQ, but I've not managed to find it anywhere. Let me know
>if I've missed it, or if there's a better group I should be posting to.

If it's a FAQ, then I've missed it too :-)

> 
>1/ My questions:
> 
>(i)	Can I interface an 8" floppy drive to a standard PC controller on
>	a PC clone computer? Do I simply need a conversion cable?

Yes, but not to read RX02's. The IBM 1.2Mbyte disk is very similar to an 8"
disk (it rotates at the same speed - 360 RPM for example), and it's trivial to
link the right signals up. I've done it several times. Now, the problem is in
data density. The original IBM PC controller could _only_ do double density,
and many clones are similar. Even those that claim single density operation are
not often reliable.
The RX01 is a standard Single-density disk. The RX02 is a very non-standard
Double density disk, with some bits of the track, like the sector headers,
being written single density I think. Nothing but a DEC RX02 drive will read an
RX02 disk, unless it was specifically designed to do it. RX01's can be read on
standard single density controllers, and can be read/written on RX02 drives


> 
>(ii)	Is there any software around (PD/commercial) which would allow me
>	to read the RX02 format (written on an RSX system, so I guess this
>	implies Files-11)? Do some PC BIOSs include 8" floppy support, and
>	would this be any help to me (since it isn't an MSDOS floppy!)

Not that I know of. You may do better to use FLX on the RSX system (I think).

> 
>(iii)	Alternatively, would it be possible for the RX02 controller on the
>	RSX host to control a 3.5" or 5.25" drive, so that only a software
>	driver would be required to read it on the PC? (ie. could the
>	RX02 controller drive, say, a 1.44MB 3.5" drive, using only 77
>	tracks instead of 80, and using 26 256-byte sectors instead of
>	18 512-byte ones?

No Way!. The RX02 'controller' is a simple bus adaptor, which sends commands
serially to the drive. The drive contains a lot of AMD bit-slice chips and
enough RAM to buffer a sector. You tell the drive to copy a sector into it's
RAM, and then to transfer the RAM to the host machine
The Drives inside the RX02 communicate with the CPU board via a special
read/write board. It's not trivial (I'll not say impossible, but I am not going
to design it!) to use any other drives with the controller

> 
>(iv)	Any other suggestions?

Yes. How about building a card for the PC that simulates the serial data link
of the RX02 bus adaptor, and then linking a real RX02 up to the PC?

