In article <oxenreid.755298294@chaos.cs.umn.edu>,
 <oxenreid@chaos.cs.umn.edu> wrote:
>Help, I am now the lowest man on the totem pole.  I have a DEC PDP-11/23
>running a weigh in motion scale in Minnesota (at my place of employment).
>Several other technicians in my shop have worked on this box, but they 
>have not solved it's problem.  So, being the UNIX person in the shop 
>( I having taken some classes in UNIX et all), I have been 'elected' to 
>assist in solving this problem.  
>
>The problem is this:  We run the computer in the shop for days, and
>we have no trouble, but when we place it in the hut at the scales, it
>does a major crash on the disk within days...

It sounds like you mean the filesystem goes bad, not the media.  Please
correct me if I'm wrong.

>I need to know :
>
>1)  How to start a maintenance shell and do a crash recovery or 
>    analysis (if such a thing exists).

Can you translate the term "maintenance shell"?  Do you mean "fire up the
diagnostics"?  Do you mean "get an RT-11 prompt"?  If you need to run real
DEC diagnostics, you need a copy of an XXDP+ disk.  If you need to access
RT-11, you can either boot from a disk that does not launch into your
application (like a minimal RT-11 floppy) or try a lot of ^C's just after
you reboot the machine.

>2)  Can I upgrade the PDP-11/23 to some other PDP-11/XX by moving some of the
>    ROM information or CPU to a new system?  [Where XX is >23]

If you don't have custom microcode, you can probably upgrade.  I have a
programmers guide for how to write Falcon microcode... looks like a pain
in the butt for little return.

You will need to give a more detailed explaination of the innards to
allow the upgrade question to be answered accurately.

>3)  Who else sells PDP-11 drive controllers (so that I can prove a point)?

Me :-)  (Lots of other people, too)  Whatcha need?  RQDX3?  RQDX1?  RLV12?
KDA50?  None of these are over a few hundred dollars on today's used market.
Bell Atlantic never charged a fair price for anything.

>4)  Is there a FAQ for these questions?

Perhaps.  I am not the person to ask on this one.

>5)  What sort of general information on PDP hardware should I know?

Everything in the DEC handbooks of the era 1980-1985 that relates to Q-bus
PDP-11 hardware.


To give accurate advice, an accurate inventory is required.  Is this a
MicroPDP (i.e. is it in a BA-23 chassis)?  What kind of disks does it
use (RL02, MFM-style RD drives, SMD)?

If it is a BA-23 chassis, check the power cable between the power supply and
the backplane.  It is to the right of the drives (as you look at the front),
behind the front panel, at the front of the power supply.  The old connector
is yellow and the wires enter the two connectors at right angles.  The new
connector is white and is actually 4 physical connectors housings (two on
each end of the cable) and the wires enter parallel to the direction of
mating.  The old cable is badly underrated and can actually burst into flame.
The basic problem is resistance leads to heat leads to charring leads to more
resistance, ad nauseum.

Good Luck,
-ethan

Remember: that box is powerful enough to run UNIX (2.9BSD)  It may be old,
but it's not obsolete.

