In article <1993Dec1.201925.29983@progress.com>, peterl@progress.COM (Peter Lauterbach) writes:

> In reading "VAX I/O Subsystems: Optimizing Performance", by Ken Bates,
> there is a section on page 94 that talks about channel card priority,
> and disk spiral rates, and getting them in the right order on the HSC.
>
> I'm working with a system that has an even mix of RA72,RA73, and RA90
> disks. Some ( most, actually ) of the RA72, and RA73s are on the higher
> priority channels, to get better response, since they are busier disks.
>
> The response rates of the drives are ok, but not great. Is there any
> way to see if this phenomena described is actually happening. (i.e.
> faster disks being locked out by slowers ones).
>
> There is also an article on DSIN that describes the same thing, but a
> conversation with a field engineer yeilded the result that, "it really
> doesn't matter". Now I'm really confused.

The HSC manual mentions prioritization (HSC version 8.0 user's guide, page
5-7). 

Attached is a note posted to DECUServe by Dale Coy which describes the
prioritization in a little bit of detail -- this was back when I was 
wondering if the prioritization was really being performed by the HSC.

The only reason I can fathom that your FE said what he said is that he
doesn't believe the change will be noticable (it probably won't if
everything is disk drives).  But the fact remains that prioritization is
done by the HSC...  Note, also, Dale's comments about tuned Backup accounts 
and the speed of the CPU and the HSC -- if these things do cause/prevent
errors from happening (and it makes sense they would), that might also 
explain why your FE says it doesn't matter.

-Dan Wing, Systems Administrator, University Hospital, Denver
 dwing@uh01.colorado.edu or wing@eisner.decus.org

-----

** Begin DECUServe note **

            <<< EISNER::$2$DIA7:[NOTES$HIVOL]HARDWARE_HELP.NOTE;1 >>>
                               -< HARDWARE_HELP >-
================================================================================
Note 1441.2         TA867 Tape Drives - Experience and hints             2 of 13
EISNER::COY "Dale E. Coy (DECUServe MoS)"            49 lines  19-JUN-1993 00:56
                        -< A long "it depends" answer >-
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
>>    8. The K.si controller board for the TA867 should be the highest
>>    Requestor (on our HSC-90, Requestor number 9).  Of course, with typical
>>    logic, the "highest requestor" is really the lowest-numbered slot that
>>    you can use. [If the tape requestor isn't the highest priority, you
>>    will almost surely see timeouts]
>
>I have heard that this does matter, and that this doesn't matter.  Is this
>documented anywhere?  I recall hearing it made a difference depending on
>which HSCxx model you had, and certain versions of the HSC software....?
    
    Indirect evidence:  
    
    Initial situation: Pair of HSC-90s, each with 2 disk requestors in the
    usual configuration, and normally with the disk "active paths" split
    between the HSCs (that is, load roughly balanced).
    
    Installed K.si for tape in a slot with lower priority than either disk
    requestor (tape requestor only on HSC0 - no redundancy yet).  Backups
    seemed to go OK, but a bit slow for my taste.
    
    "ReTuned" backup account for new situation (mostly increased WSDEF). 
    Started getting strange errors (something like "Tape device not
    asserting ready when expected").  SICL kindly sent these via e-mail to
    the CSC.
    
    FS moved tape requestor to Requestor number 9.  This error quit
    appearing.  Then I failed-over *ALL* disk drives to the same HSC (that
    is - the one with the tape, too).  And a complete backup ran without
    giving the error.
    
>I have heard that this does matter, and that this doesn't matter.  Is this
>documented anywhere?  I recall hearing it made a difference depending on
>which HSCxx model you had, and certain versions of the HSC software....?
    
    Other evidence: the same thing used to happen to us (elsewhere) with a
    TA-78 on an HSC-50, with RA-81 drives - until we moved the tape
    requestor to the highest priority.
    
    I am sure there is a relationship with the HSC model (how fast it is),
    and my experience says that if your Backup account isn't tuned very
    well you won't see the problem (often) either.  And I am guessing that
    there is a relationship with the speed of the CPU running Backup and
    with the transfer speed of the disks being backed up.
    
    I think I recall some "old" articles on DSIN (and/or DSNlink) -
    probably about the TA78 and/or TA79.
    
    The general subject of requestor priority is discussed in the HSC
    manual.

** End DECUServe note **


