In article <1993Nov30.220634.3332@phx.mcd.mot.com>, henryw@phx.mcd.mot.com (Henry Wong) writes...
>I read in the December 93 issue of IEEE Spectrum that power dissipation
>of the 200 MHz Alpha 21064 is 30 Watts.  What kind of cooling technology
>is required?  What does the heat sink look like?  Any comments or references
>would be greatly appreciated.
> 
The heat sink is machined aluminum, with fins about 1.5" tall aligned to
the direction of the airflow.  The faster the flow, the shorter the fins
can be.

In the DECpc 150 AXP, an Alpha chip at 150Mhz is cooled using only the
standard fans which come with the enclosure (originally used for an Intel
PC).  The only design issue was to make sure that airflow to the heat
sink was unobstructed.  Tests have shown that a 200Mhz chip can be cooled
in this enclosure.

Re a different thread: sorry, the press reports were wrong.  The Alpha
notebook demo shown at Comdex had a 21066 chip running at 130Mhz; a
different demo unit, of a PC-class system, had a 21064A chip running
at 320Mhz.

