In article <1993Nov30.183248.15973@Princeton.EDU>, mfterman@tucson.Princeton.EDU (Mutant for Hire) writes:

|> The things is, up until now, the differences between workstations and
|> PCs has been primarily architectural, in the sense of what operating
|> systems they run, and the type of video they produce. The difference
|> these days is in the difference that the PC will run with less speed but
|> with less cost as well, while the workstation is purchased more for pure
|> performance. There is no dividing characteristic between PC's and
|> workstations. You can say "fast CPU's" but how fast is fast in this case?
|> The same goes for lots of RAM and disk.

I only excerpted the above to give an indication of what I am responding to.
There have been other similar posts. I work in an environment with both
workstations and PCs, and I know a couple of computer equipment brokers, and
others in the industry. I've asked around, and I think there still _are_
dividing lines. ANd, since 85% or so of all "PC's" (or is it all computers?)
are ISA-architecture (x86) systems, I won't try to wrap up Macs and such,
even though I suspect much is the same...

MEMORY: typical range of PC's is 4MB to 16MB. Typical range for workstations
        is 32MB to 256MB (and more)
DISK: typical range is from 80MB to 350MB for PC's, or 512MB to 4GB for
      workstations.
DISPLAY: Pc's typically go from 800x600 today up to 1280x1024, while workstations
         are typically starting at 1280x1024 and go from there.

That's it! Note that everything mentioned is "typical". Of course there will
be systems that fall outside those ranges, but they are few and far between
compared to the rest of the machines out there. Note I don't mention speed,
because I don't see a big difference anymore (most of the workstations lying
around which aren't new are no faster than today's off-the-shelf PC's) except
at the extremes. I also don't talk about networking, since this is hardly
a distinguishing characteristics. Nor do I mention bus interfaces, since both
PC's and workstations have low-end dogs, and high-end screamers.

BUT, my point is that I believe, and everyone I've talked to does as well,
that you can easily use the above criteria to distinguish between a "typical"
PC and workstation. Price is also a good indicator, but is only a symptom,
not a root difference. If you were to compare them, though, you'd find about
an order of magnitude difference. Typical PC's are in the lower thousands (1-3),
while typical workstations are in the lower tens of thousands. For example,
if I wanted a "decent" PC I'd pay no more than about $1200 for a 486SX system
in the above "typical" ranges. If I wanted a "decent" workstation I'd probably
pay $10,000. I'd pay a lot more for either for high-end configurations. The
price disparity is already shrinking, though, so I won't claim it's an absolute
crucible for telling the difference.

Well, that's my $.02 worth. Here in the industry, there _is_ a difference.
Muse all you want about the theoritical similarities and look at where the
two worlds overlap, but it won't change the reality that there are still two
distinct, separate classes.

