In article <4771@sparky.mdavcr.mda.ca>, fieldt@mdavcr.mda.ca (Terry Field) writes:
>I am trying to find a solution to the following problem:
>
>	I want to run special-to-type software (VAX/VMS Pascal
>	primarily) on a single board CPU, interfacing to a
>	Unix host.
>
>The optimum would be a single CPU card, running VMS, which 'plugs 
>into' the Unix box with VME/S-Bus as an interface, although an
>external solution may also be acceptable, as well as multiple cards.  
>Does this type of card exist? Who supplies it and how do I get hold
>of more information?
>
>All help/suggestions in this regard would be greatly appreciated.

There are no SBC's available running VMS, but there is the KAV-30, a VME board
with a uVAX II chip on it. This runs VAX ELN, but the ELN linker accepts VMS
object code, so you can port most VMS code to it fairly easily.

The "native" language for ELN is its own flavour of Pascal, somewhat different
from the VMS flavour.

The KAV-30 is available from Digital, though you might have to educate your
sales person about its existence. It is handled by CSS.

I believe there is a 3rd party producing a faster VME VAX board.

An easier, probably cheaper solution (especially if you really need VMS) would 
be a VAXstation 4000 VLC system box communicating via Ethernet or SCSI. If you
want a supported solution, look for a MicroVAX 3100, Model 30 or better. You
should be able to pick one up for a reasonable price, but you will  pay more
