This is exactly the point. Users don't get work done by running the specint
and specfp benchmarks, they get work done by running APPLICATIONS. If native
applications are available promptly and upgraded at the same time as the 
X86/Windows then they will get market share. What drives the software market
is volume. Look at the applications available for Windows 3.1.  Compare 
the features with anything you can get for Unix (no fair comparing
a port from Windows like Excel; what applications came out originally on
Unix ?) Now compare the cost. If Windows 3.1 <-> WindowsNT becomes a trivial
port, and the cost of NT comes down to the point where desktop volumes are
involved, then we might see the migration start to happen. If the PowerPC
remains linked to AIX and System 7, with Windows emulation, then they will
remain niche products. Yes I know about API's and that Insignia has a source
code license for Windows. There are fantasic things possible, what has been
