In article <CHB919.Fzp@biles.com> daniels@biles.com (Brad Daniels) writes:
>In article <3525@niktow.canisius.edu>,
>Greg Pavlov <pavlov@niktow.canisius.edu> wrote:
>...
>>  But the Alpha accomplishes this by running at twice the clock rate and more.
>>  IBM accomplishes the same thing by generally adhering to "RISC" concepts but
[...]
>This is exactly where the problem lies with Pentium.  As you state later,
>Pentium was a major accomplishment *given its constraints*.  Those constraints
>are very severe, however.  I will be extremely surprised if I *ever* see a
>Pentium running over 200 MHz, while the Alpha can already run well over 300
>MHz.  The Alpha architecture is designed to work with high clock speeds.

But can you keep increasing clock rate forever, and won't you hit a
limitation in the speed of DRAM and cache? A lot of applications use
huge arrays, much bigger than can fit in any practical size of cache.
Main DRAM won't cycle at more than 20 or 30 MHz, and SRAM capable of 
keeping up with a 300 MHz chip must be exceedingly expensive and must
produce a lot of heat.

The recent BYTE benchmarks showed that a DEC 300 with a mere 64-bit bus
and "small" 256k L2 cache, with the alpha running at 150 MHz, performed
simple floating point operations at exactly the same speed as a 60 MHz
pentium. Perhaps these are inaccurate benchmarks, but there is a strong
hint that Intel doesn't need to go to much more than 100 MHz to be
competitive.



