	We have an interesting problem, to which the solution would appear
to be to use a C++/OO approach... but we have one major concern EFFICIENCY??

The problem : We have a fairly large (about 75,000 lines) application
	      that needs to be re-architected and re-coded.  The basic
	      attributes of the application are as follows:
		* Near real-time data processing
		* Rapid prototype spaghetti code (VMS Fortran)
		* Runs primarily in a DEC VMS environment
		* Many processes with gobs of shared memory
		* Data crunching vs data management is split 30/70 in lines of code
		* Data crunching vs data management is split 80/20 in CPU usage
		* 100% utilization of current processing power

	We believe strongly in all the benefits of C++/OO, especially
portability, since we will eventually need to be able to run our system 
on different hosts.  But weUll also be out of a job if we deliver
a product that cannot process at least the same data rates as the 
current prototype system.  

	Some of our preliminary benchmarks indicate there may be a significant
performance hit for going to C++... does anyone out there have experience with 
this kind of conversion excersize, or have any insights into what we should do?

	Ideas we are toying with include:
		* Trashing the whole C++ experiment in favor of speedy Fortran
		* Building our main system in C++, using Fortran for CPU hogs
		* Switching to ADA instead (just kidding!)


	References to papers on previous experiences, personal dumps, diatriabs...

	We'll take anything...

			Thanks
				Dirk Manson


