I wish I could afford the spec suite, but I can't. I'm not sure I can
believe either Intel's or DEC's benchmarks. The benchmarks I have seen
elsewhere point to a much smaller difference in FP speed. If someone
would post the Alpha PC's results for c-linpack, it would help me out
with a more concrete example, relevant to my work (I use linpack
routines).

Here's some grist for the mill. I ran the straight clinpack benchmark,
double precision, on a less-than-optimal 60 MHz pentium with 256K
write-through cache. The compiler was symmantec's C++, which does no
pentium optimizations (save 8-byte alignment). The result was 6.4 MFLOPS
linpack (please distinguish from whatever other measure of "FLOPS" might
come to mind). With very minor recoding of the BLAS routines, to
simulate a very good compiler, it gets about 9.9 MFLOPS double linpack.
Increasing the arrays to 800x800 and order n = 400, the rating is 7.3 or
so (these arrays are large enough to flood the cache).

Can someone out there run the same benchmark on an alpha PC, including
the case where the cache is flooded (important, because the standard
c-linpack is too small)? I'll try to post the benchmark to this group,
or mail it to you if you can't get it elsewhere.

I can run the benchmarks on a very high-end alpha, but I doubt the
results would be too meanigful, since that system has a lot of cache.

>Are you _sure_ Pentium boxes with a similar configuration (including
>the EISA slots) costs $4000?

I wouldn't touch EISA with a ten-foot pole. The configuration I
described before, with a 17" monitor and twice the hard disk of the
alpha PC, and a graphics adapter that is twice as fast as Q-vision, plus
double speed CD-ROM, is $3995. The "Best buy" model is $3000 (it has 8
MB instead of 16, 340MB hard drive instead of 520MB, smaller monitor).
 I have the $4 K model.
These systems are PCI, not EISA.

I note you suggest $300-400 for a monitor. That would be a 14" or
low-end 15". Note 17" monitors cost more like $900 to $1200. I've become
quite enamored with 17" monitors; never thought I would, but it makes
a fair difference in my eye strain.


