  But the Alpha accomplishes this by running at twice the clock rate and more.
  IBM accomplishes the same thing by generally adhering to "RISC" concepts but
  implementing them via a complex chip set (POWER and POWER2).  MIPS does the
  same via clock-doubling.  HP, for one reason or another, has either chosen
  not to or failed to implement on-chip cache.

Alpha's clock rate is high because a lot of hardware design decisions were
made in favour of the high clock rate. IBM's POWER2 hardware architecture
would be impossible to produce at the Alpha's clock rate given today's
technology. The Alpha's performance, although linked to it's clock rate, is
by no means limited. (Look for 300MHz Alpha's next year.) IBM's POWER2
architecture takes advantage of having a large "processor bed" by providing
a number of independant functional units (it's potentially capable of
issuing 6 instructions simultaneously I believe). MIPS's performance is not
from clock doubling (which is better described IMHO as clock halving,
because it effectively reduces the power of a high-speed CPU by placing it
a bus that is slower than theoretical optimum). MIPS's performance is
linked to the fact that the R4k architecture is DEAPLY pipelined. This has
actually become a bit of a performance hinderence, but early news on the
R4600 and R4200 indicates they are moving away from this problem. The HP
platform utilizes an external L1 cache and no L2 cache. This is based on
the principle that L2 caches are not cost effective (yet to be proven, but
also yet to be proven wrong). The HP architecture allows HP to have L1
caches with sizes of 4 MB instead of say 16 KB as in the Pentium. HP's
technology in particular should be well suited to multi-processing systems.

  So on the surface, it appears to me that as each architecture is pushed to 
  higher performance, distinct problems will have to be faced and overcome. 
  HP may be an exception - the cache "problem" may not be a problem.  Power PC
  may be an exception as well, at least for the time being.

Most RISC architectures have yet to hit a real "problem" that has to be
overcome, with the possible exception of Sun's SPARC chip (and even that
one I'd say still has a fair bit of room to grow). Sure they've hit limits,
but if they *weren't* at limits, they wouldn't be in the workstation
market, and if they weren't moving those limits CONSTANTLY they wouldn't be
in the workstation market for long.

  CPU design ain't easy and everyone is faced with many tradeoffs, some on the
  surface (as above) and many more which are hidden.

True enough.

  Whatever one may think of Intel and the x86 architecture, it appears to me
  that the Pentium was a major accomplishment given the restraints that had to
  be faced, most prominent being backward compatibility.  To me, anyway, it is
  at least on a par with what DEC accomplished with the VAX architecture in the
  last 3 years.  My suspicion is that in both cases, a lot of people in a lot
  of other companies were surprised - and perhaps caught off guard - by the 
  results.  

DEC's accomplishments with the VAX architecture, although impressive, by no
means avoided the fact that the architecture was reaching it's end. That's
why we have Alpha's today. DEC knew they needed a completely new platform
if they were going to continue on into the future. I think Intel realizes
this too, but they are holding off for at least one more generation. I
don't think anyone has been particularly suprised by the Pentium. Heck,
Motorola's 68060 is doing the same in the lab's (the only difference being
that the 68k market doesn't have enough $$'s in it to justify opening a fab
that isn't ready for the chip).

